e": 1, "language": "markdown"}], "work_started_at": "2026-10-05T06:47:41.343632", "work_ended_at": "2026-10-05T06:47:47.490001"}, "created_at": "2026-10-05T06:47:47.513Z", "thread_id": "chat_thread_b7c56080011c5d78", "workspace_scope_id": "chat_scope_4251f2bdb6c2290c", "run_id": "9d4de86e-c224-4704-9dae-9ed144c62b77", "route": "general", "conversation_category": "general", "artifact_paths": {}, "selected_nodes": [], "dataset_refs": []}, {"id": 9128, "role": "user", "content": {"message": "Analyze this dataset and identify missing values and unusual patterns", "attached_datasets": [], "selected_nodes": [], "selected_node_details": [], "job_id": null}, "created_at": "2026-10-05T06:49:00.742Z", "thread_id": "chat_thread_b7c56080011c5d78", "workspace_scope_id": "chat_scope_4251f2bdb6c2290c", "run_id": "eaffcebb-eab6-43bc-bc87-8c1fe7f0dea3", "route": null, "conversation_category": "general", "artifact_paths": {}, "selected_nodes": [], "dataset_refs": []}, {"id": 9129, "role": "assistant", "content": {"status": "success", "route": "general", "message": "All data collected. Now I'll write the full analysis report.\n\n---\n\n## Data Quality & Pattern Analysis \u2014 `baseline.csv`\n\n### Introduction\n\nThis report examines the `baseline.csv` dataset, which records customer orders spanning January 2025 through August 2\n\n026. The dataset contains 1,050 rows across eight fields \u2014 `order_id`, `customer_name`, `email`, `age`, `country`, `order_date`, `amount_usd`, and `status` \u2014 and appears to represent an e-commerce or transaction log intended to track order-level customer activity across multiple countries. The analysis was undertaken to identify data quality problems and unusual patterns that would compromise the reliability of any downstream analysis or reporting built on this dataset.\n\nThe investigation reveals a dataset with pervasive, multi-layered quality issues. Missing values, duplicate records, formatting inconsistencies, extreme numeric outliers, and widespread date parsing failures each represent separate, co-existing problems. Individually, any one of these would be a manageable concern; together, they indicate that the dataset requires systematic remediation before it can be trusted for operational or analytical use.\n\n---\n\n###\n\n1. Missing Values\n\nChart 1 shows the missing value profile across all eight fields. Three fields contain missing entries: `amount_usd` (60 missing, 5.7%), `email` (57 missing, 5.4%), and `country` (45 missing, 4.3%). The remaining five fields \u2014 `order_id`, `customer_name`, `age`, `order_date`, and `status` \u2014 are fully populated.\n\n![Missing Values by Field](artifact://chart/missing_values)\n\n| Field | Missing Count | Missing % |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| amount_usd | 60 | 5.7% |\n| email | 57 | 5.4% |\n| country | 45 | 4.3% |\n| All other fields | 0 | 0.0% |\n\nThe concentration of missing values in `amount_usd` is the most operationally significant issue. An order record without a transaction amount is analytically incomplete \u2014 it cannot contribute to revenue calculations, average order value metrics, or financial summaries. The 60 missing amounts represent roughly 5.7% of the dataset, which is a meaningful share when the total volume is only 1,050 rows. The missing `email` values are similarly problematic in any context where customer outreach or deduplication across records would depend on this field. Missing `country` values (45 records) undermine any geographic segmentation or regional analysis. The pattern of missingness is not obviously systematic, but its distribution across three different fields suggests it may have originated from multiple upstream collection points rather than a single ingestion failure.\n\n---\n\n###\n\n2. Duplicate Records and Order ID Integrity\n\nThe dataset contains 1,050 rows but only 1,000 distinct `order_id` values, meaning **50 rows share an `order_id` with at least one other row**. This is a fundamental integrity problem. An order ID is conventionally a unique transaction identifier, and its duplication suggests either that the same transaction was ingested more than once, that IDs were recycled or reused, or that the source system generating these records has an upstream deduplication failure.\n\nThe practical implication is significant. Any analysis that sums revenue, counts transactions, or tracks customer behaviour at the order level will double-count some records unless duplicates are identified and resolved. Without knowing whether the duplicated rows represent genuinely separate transactions or erroneous copies, it is not safe to simply drop them \u2014 the correct resolution depends on whether the duplicated rows contain matching or differing field values. This should be one of the first issues addressed in any data cleaning pipeline.\n\n---\n\n###\n\n3. Date Field: Widespread Parsing Failures\n\nThe `order_date` field shows zero null values, which at first glance suggests complete date coverage. However, when dates are parsed through standard ISO date conversion (`try_cast`), only **274 of 1,050 rows yield a valid date** \u2014 meaning approximately **776 rows (74%) contain unparseable date strings**. This is the single most severe data quality finding in the dataset.\n\nFrom the monthly aggregation of valid dates, orders span January 2025 to August 2026, a reasonable 20-month window. The 274 records that do parse cover all 20 months relatively evenly (9\u201319 orders per month). The remaining 776 records almost certainly contain dates in one or more alternative formats \u2014 such as `DD/MM/YYYY`, `MM-DD-YYYY`, `YYYY/MM/DD`, or locale-specific representations \u2014 that are inconsistent with the expected `YYYY-MM-DD` format. This is not a small formatting edge case; it affects three-quarters of the dataset, which means the date field as-stored is not analytically usable in its current form without format standardisation.\n\nThe consequence is that any time-series analysis, trend reporting, cohort analysis, or date-based filtering based on this field would silently exclude the majority of records. This is especially dangerous because the excluded records do not raise visible errors \u2014 they simply disappear from date-filtered queries.\n\n---\n\n###\n\n4. Numeric Outliers and Amount Anomalies\n\nThe `amount_usd` field contains several categories of unusual values, as shown in Charts 4 and 5.\n\n![Order Amount Distribution](artifact://chart/amount_distribution)\n\n**Extreme high outlier:** The maximum recorded amount is **$9,999,999** \u2014 orders of magnitude above the typical range. The vast majority of orders fall in the $100\u2013$500 band (773 out of the ~990 records with valid amounts), making a $10M individual order clearly anomalous. This single value is likely a data entry error, a test record, or a sentinel value used to represent a system error. Its presence inflates the dataset's average order value to approximately **$70,955**, a figure that bears no resemblance to the modal transaction range of $200\u2013$500. The April 2025 and September 2025 monthly revenue spikes visible in Chart 4 \u2014 where revenue jumps to ~$10M and ~$30M respectively against an otherwise consistent $2,000\u2013$5,500/month baseline \u2014 are almost certainly caused by one or two such extreme-value records landing in those months.\n\n![Monthly Orders & Revenue Trend](artifact://chart/monthly_trend)\n\n**Negative amounts:** 9 records carry a negative `amount_usd` value (minimum: \u2013$49.99). In some systems, negative amounts legitimately represent refunds or reversals, but in the context of this dataset \u2014 where `status` already encodes refund states \u2014 their presence without a clear definitional convention is ambiguous and potentially duplicative of the `refunded` status field.\n\n**Zero amounts:** 5 records have `amount_usd = 0`. A zero-value order is unusual and may indicate a free promotion, a data collection failure, or a test transaction.\n\n**Invalid age values:** The `age` field registers a minimum of \u20135 and a maximum of 999, with 20 records falling outside the plausible human range of 1\u2013\n\n120. These values are almost certainly entry errors. While age statistics (mean ~52) appear broadly plausible for the valid portion of the data, the presence of invalid extremes means age-based segmentation or filtering must account for these outliers explicitly.\n\n---\n\n###\n\n5. Formatting and Consistency Inconsistencies\n\nTwo fields \u2014 `country` and `customer_name` \u2014 exhibit pronounced formatting inconsistencies that prevent reliable grouping and aggregation.\n\n![Orders by Country](artifact://chart/orders_by_country)\n\n**Country fragmentation:** The country field contains the same country name in multiple capitalisation variants and with embedded whitespace. For example, \"Brazil\" appears as at minimum `brazil`, `Brazil`, `BRAZIL`, and `Brazil ` (with a trailing space), each of which is treated as a distinct value by any grouping query. The same pattern appears for Canada, Vietnam, Nigeria, Australia, and India. As Table 3 shows, this results in each country being split into 3\u20135 separate groups, making any geographic count or revenue analysis by country deeply unreliable without normalisation.\n\n| True Country | Observed Variants | Total Records (approx.) |\n|---|---|---:|\n| Brazil | brazil, Brazil, BRAZIL, \"Brazil \" | ~182 |\n| Canada | canada, Canada, CANADA, \"Canada \" | ~187 |\n| Vietnam | vietnam, Vietnam, VIETNAM, \"Vietnam \" | ~164 |\n| Nigeria | nigeria, Nigeria, NIGERIA, \"Nigeria \" | ~168 |\n| Australia | australia, Australia, AUSTRALIA, \"Australia \" | ~176 |\n| India | india, India, INDIA | ~128 |\n\n**Customer name casing:** Of the 1,050 name records, 276 are entered in ALL CAPS and 237 are entirely lowercase, with the remainder in mixed case. The sample rows confirmed this directly \u2014 \"elena patel\", \"HIRO KIM\", and \"Ivy Smith\" all appear in the same dataset. This inconsistency makes reliable customer deduplication or name-matching impossible without normalisation, and it also suggests the data was collected from multiple sources or entry points with no consistent formatting rules.\n\n**Email validity:** 18 records contain an `email` value that does not include the `@` character \u2014 the minimum structural requirement for a valid email address. These are definitionally invalid contact records and should be flagged for correction or exclusion from any customer communication workflows.\n\n---\n\n###\n\n6. Order Status Distribution\n\n![Order Status Distribution](artifact://chart/status_distribution)\n\nThe `status` field is evenly distributed across three values: `refunded` (383, 36.5%), `pending` (334, 31.8%), and `paid` (333, 31.7%). The near-equal three-way split is itself a notable pattern. In a typical order dataset, `paid` would be expected to dominate, with `refunded` and `pending` as smaller exception categories. Having refunded orders as the single largest group \u2014 marginally exceeding paid orders \u2014 is unusual and may reflect either that the dataset is a sample deliberately constructed to include quality-edge cases, or that the business has a genuinely high refund rate that warrants investigation. The average order value for `paid` records ($97,964) is significantly higher than for `refunded` ($55,504) or `pending` ($65,400), which is distorted by the extreme outlier values discussed above and should not be interpreted at face value.\n\n---\n\n### Recommendations\n\n**\n\n1. Resolve duplicate order IDs before any downstream use.** The 50 duplicate IDs represent a structural integrity failure that will produce inflated counts and revenue figures in any analysis. The remediation approach \u2014 whether deduplication or investigation of whether these are genuinely separate transactions \u2014 depends on a cross-check against the source system.\n\n**\n\n2. Standardise the `order_date` field format urgently.** With 74% of date records unparseable, this field is effectively unusable for time-series analysis. The dataset owner should identify the mix of date formats in use and apply a consistent parsing and storage standard. Downstream pipelines should validate and reject malformed dates at ingestion.\n\n**\n\n3. Normalise the `country` and `customer_name` fields.** All country values should be uppercased or lowercased, trimmed of whitespace, and mapped to a canonical country list. Customer names should be stored in a consistent casing convention. Without this, geographic and customer-level aggregations will produce fragmented, misleading results.\n\n**\n\n4. Remove or flag the extreme `amount_usd` outlier.** The $9,999,999 value distorts all amount-level statistics and revenue figures. It should be flagged for manual review and, if confirmed as erroneous, removed or corrected before any revenue analysis is run. Negative and zero amounts should also be classified explicitly \u2014 whether they represent valid refunds, test records, or errors.\n\n**\n\n5. Validate and correct out-of-range `age` values.** The 20 records with ages \u2264 0 or \u2265 121 should be flagged for correction. Any age-based analytics should apply a validity filter (e.g., age between 1 and 110) until the field is cleaned.\n\n**\n\n6. Investigate the high refund rate.** The near-equal split between refunded, pending, and paid orders is atypical and merits a business-level explanation. If this reflects real operational behaviour rather than a sampling artefact, the underlying drivers of high refunds warrant their own investigation.\n\n---\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe `baseline.csv` dataset contains a substantial volume of data quality issues spanning every major problem category: missing values, duplicate keys, invalid formats, extreme outliers, and systematic inconsistencies. No single field is entirely clean, and several issues \u2014 particularly the date parsing failures affecting 74% of records and the country fragmentation rendering geographic analysis unreliable \u2014 are severe enough to make the dataset unsuitable for production analysis without remediation. The most important immediate priority is to establish a data cleaning pipeline that addresses date format standardisation, order ID deduplication, country name normalisation, and outlier flagging as its first four steps. Until those issues are resolved, any aggregated metrics \u2014 revenue totals, order counts, geographic breakdowns, or trend analyses \u2014 derived from this dataset carry a high risk of being materially incorrect.", "suggestions": ["Tell me your concrete goal in one sentence", "Share whether you want build, analysis, or debug", "If needed, I can call a worker next"], "handled_by": "session_main_agent_sdk_direct_stream", "executed_code": "import sys, json\nsys.path.insert(0, '/mnt/rhombus-analysis/rhombo_data_analysis_sessions/production/session_3cec8906d40e5a87')\nfrom rhombus_data import query_sources\nimport pandas as pd\nimport plotly.graph_objects as go\nimport plotly.express as px\nfrom plotly.utils import PlotlyJSONEncoder\n\nsource = {\"source\": \"baseline\"}\n\n# Single comprehensive query combining all needed aggregations\nsql = \"\"\"\nWITH base AS (\n  SELECT\n    order_id,\n    customer_name,\n    email,\n    age,\n    country,\n    order_date,\n    amount_usd,\n    status,\n    -- Flag nulls/blanks\n    CASE WHEN order_id IS NULL OR TRIM(order_id) = '' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS null_order_id,\n    CASE WHEN customer_name IS NULL OR TRIM(customer_name) = '' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS null_name,\n    CASE WHEN email IS NULL OR TRIM(email) = '' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS null_email,\n    CASE WHEN age IS NULL OR TRIM(age) = '' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS null_age,\n    CASE WHEN country IS NULL OR TRIM(country) = '' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS null_country,\n    CASE WHEN order_date IS NULL OR TRIM(order_date) = '' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS null_order_date,\n    CASE WHEN amount_usd IS NULL OR TRIM(amount_usd) = '' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS null_amount,\n    CASE WHEN status IS NULL OR TRIM(status) = '' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END AS null_status,\n    -- Parsed numeric values\n    try_cast(age AS double) AS age_num,\n    try_cast(amount_usd AS double) AS amount_num,\n    try_cast(order_date AS date) AS order_date_parsed\n  FROM source\n),\ntotals AS (\n  SELECT\n    COUNT(*) AS total_rows,\n    COUNT(DISTINCT order_id) AS distinct_orders,\n    SUM(null_order_id) AS miss_order_id,\n    SUM(null_name) AS miss_name,\n    SUM(null_email) AS miss_email,\n    SUM(null_age) AS miss_age,\n    SUM(null_country) AS miss_country,\n    SUM(null_order_date) AS miss_order_date,\n    SUM(null_amount) AS miss_amount,\n    SUM(null_status) AS miss_status,\n    -- Age stats\n    MIN(age_num) AS age_min,\n    MAX(age_num) AS age_max,\n    AVG(age_num) AS age_avg,\n    -- Amount stats\n    MIN(amount_num) AS amount_min,\n    MAX(amount_num) AS amount_max,\n    AVG(amount_num) AS amount_avg,\n    SUM(amount_num) AS amount_total,\n    -- Unusual: negative amounts\n    SUM(CASE WHEN amount_num < 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS negative_amounts,\n    -- Unusual: zero amounts\n    SUM(CASE WHEN amount_num = 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS zero_amounts,\n    -- Unusual: invalid age (<=0 or >120)\n    SUM(CASE WHEN age_num <= 0 OR age_num > 120 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS invalid_age,\n    -- Duplicate order_ids\n    COUNT(*) - COUNT(DISTINCT order_id) AS duplicate_order_ids,\n    -- Invalid date parse\n    SUM(CASE WHEN order_date IS NOT NULL AND TRIM(order_date) != '' AND order_date_parsed IS NULL THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS invalid_dates,\n    -- Name casing inconsistency count\n    SUM(CASE WHEN customer_name = UPPER(customer_name) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS all_caps_names,\n    SUM(CASE WHEN customer_name = LOWER(customer_name) THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS all_lower_names,\n    -- Email without @\n    SUM(CASE WHEN email NOT LIKE '%@%' AND email IS NOT NULL AND TRIM(email) != '' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS invalid_emails,\n    -- Date range\n    MIN(order_date_parsed) AS date_min,\n    MAX(order_date_parsed) AS date_max\n  FROM base\n),\nstatus_counts AS (\n  SELECT status, COUNT(*) AS cnt FROM base GROUP BY status\n),\ncountry_counts AS (\n  SELECT country, COUNT(*) AS cnt FROM base GROUP BY country ORDER BY cnt DESC\n),\nmonthly AS (\n  SELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', order_date_parsed) AS month, COUNT(*) AS orders, SUM(amount_num) AS revenue\n  FROM base WHERE order_date_parsed IS NOT NULL\n  GROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1\n),\namount_buckets AS (\n  SELECT\n    CASE\n      WHEN amount_num < 0 THEN 'Negative'\n      WHEN amount_num = 0 THEN 'Zero'\n      WHEN amount_num < 50 THEN '0-50'\n      WHEN amount_num < 100 THEN '50-100'\n      WHEN amount_num < 200 THEN '100-200'\n      WHEN amount_num < 500 THEN '200-500'\n      ELSE '500+'\n    END AS bucket,\n    COUNT(*) AS cnt\n  FROM base GROUP BY 1\n)\nSELECT 'totals' AS qtype, to_json(CAST(ROW(\n  total_rows, distinct_orders, miss_order_id, miss_name, miss_email,\n  miss_age, miss_country, miss_order_date, miss_amount, miss_status,\n  age_min, age_max, age_avg, amount_min, amount_max, amount_avg, amount_total,\n  negative_amounts, zero_amounts, invalid_age, duplicate_order_ids,\n  invalid_dates, all_caps_names, all_lower_names, invalid_emails, date_min, date_max\n) AS ROW(\n  total_rows bigint, distinct_orders bigint, miss_order_id bigint, miss_name bigint, miss_email bigint,\n  miss_age bigint, miss_country bigint, miss_order_date bigint, miss_amount bigint, miss_status bigint,\n  age_min double, age_max double, age_avg double, amount_min double, amount_max double, amount_avg double, amount_total double,\n  negative_amounts bigint, zero_amounts bigint, invalid_age bigint, duplicate_order_ids bigint,\n  invalid_dates bigint, all_caps_names bigint, all_lower_names bigint, invalid_emails bigint, date_min varchar, date_max varchar\n))) AS data\nFROM totals\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'status' AS qtype, CAST(status AS varchar) || ':' || CAST(cnt AS varchar) AS data FROM status_counts\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'country' AS qtype, CAST(country AS varchar) || ':' || CAST(cnt AS varchar) AS data FROM country_counts\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'monthly' AS qtype, CAST(month AS varchar) || ':' || CAST(orders AS varchar) || ':' || CAST(ROUND(revenue,2) AS varchar) AS data FROM monthly\nUNION ALL\nSELECT 'bucket' AS qtype, bucket || ':' || CAST(cnt AS varchar) AS data FROM amount_buckets\n\"\"\"\n\ndf = query_sources(source, sql)\nprint(df.to_string())\n", "agent_version": "main_session_claude_sdk", "execution_path": "main_session_workspace", "artifact_paths": {"analysis_py": "/mnt/rhombus-analysis/rhombo_data_analysis_sessions/production/session_3cec8906d40e5a87/rhombus_output/analysis.py", "python_script": "/mnt/rhombus-analysis/rhombo_data_analysis_sessions/production/session_3cec8906d40e5a87/rhombus_output/analysis.py", "python_script_analysis": "/mnt/rhombus-analysis/rhombo_data_analysis_sessions/production/session_3cec8906d40e5a87/rhombus_output/analysis.py", "python_script_schema_probe": "/mnt/rhombus-analysis/rhombo_data_analysis_sessions/production/session_3cec8906d40e5a87/rhombus_output/schema_probe.py", "amount_distribution": "/mnt/rhombus-analysis/rhombo_data_analysis_sessions/production/session_3cec8906d40e5a87/rhombus_output/charts/amount_distribution.plotly.json", "missing_values": "/mnt/rhombus-analysis/rhombo_data_analysis_sessions/production/session_3cec8906d40e5a87/rhombus_output/charts/missing_values.plotly.json", "monthly_trend": "/mnt/rhombus-analysis/rhombo_data_analysis_sessions/production/session_3cec8906d40e5a87/rhombus_output/charts/monthly_trend.plotly.json", "orders_by_country": "/mnt/rhombus-analysis/rhombo_data_analysis_sessions/production/session_3cec8906d40e5a87/rhombus_output/charts/orders_by_country.plotly.json", "status_distribution": "/mnt/rhombus-analysis/rhombo_data_analysis_sessions/production/session_3cec8906d40e5a87/rhombus_output/charts/status_distribution.plotly.json"}, "artifact_refs": [{"key": "analysis_py